      <button type="button" class="ks-compare__thumb" data-sample="1" aria-label="Sample 2"></button>
      <button type="button" class="ks-compare__thumb" data-sample="2" aria-label="Sample 3"></button>
      <button type="button" class="ks-compare__thumb" data-sample="3" aria-label="Sample 4"></button>
    </div>
    <p class="tw:mt-[16px] tw:text-center tw:text-[13px] tw:text-neutral-600">Pick a frame to compare · real comparison scans drop into these slots</p>
  </section>

  {%- comment -%} ==================== WHY RGB (crosstalk explainer) ==================== {%- endcomment -%}
  <section id="why-rgb" class="tw:mx-auto tw:w-full tw:max-w-[1400px] tw:scroll-mt-[84px] tw:px-[15px] tw:py-[80px] tw:md:px-[50px] tw:md:py-[120px]">
    <div class="tw:mx-auto tw:max-w-[760px] tw:text-center">
      <p class="tw:mb-[16px] tw:text-[13px] tw:font-semibold tw:uppercase tw:tracking-[0.16em] tw:text-[#60a5fa]">Why RGB</p>
      <h2 class="tw:text-[32px] tw:font-semibold tw:leading-[1.1] tw:tracking-[-0.02em] tw:text-white tw:md:text-[46px]">The problem isn't your camera — it's the light.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="tw:mt-[48px] tw:grid tw:grid-cols-1 tw:items-center tw:gap-[48px] tw:md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <p class="tw:text-[17px] tw:leading-[1.65] tw:text-neutral-400">Colour negatives aren't made to be viewed under white light. They're three stacked dye layers — cyan, magenta and yellow — built to be printed onto paper that reads light in three narrow bands. Scan them with white light and your sensor records all three layers at once. That blending is called <span class="tw:text-neutral-200">crosstalk</span>, and it's what causes cyan skies, magenta shadows and skin tones that never look quite right.</p>
        <p class="tw:mt-[20px] tw:text-[17px] tw:leading-[1.65] tw:text-neutral-400">toneLight fixes it at the source. It takes three exposures through narrowband red, green and blue light — one per dye layer — so each channel is read cleanly. Inversions get easier, colours more predictable, and the look holds across the whole roll. It's how lab and cinema scanners have worked for decades; now your existing rig can too.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        {%- comment -%} R/G/B channels merge then convert on scroll (reused component; assets already in /assets) {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="ts-rgb">
          <div class="ts-rgb__stage">
            <div class="ts-rgb__ch ts-rgb__ch--r"></div>
            <div class="ts-rgb__ch ts-rgb__ch--g"></div>
            <div class="ts-rgb__ch ts-rgb__ch--b"></div>
            <div class="ts-rgb__result"></div>
          </div>
          <span class="ts-rgb__cap">Three RGB channel captures</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%} spectral crosstalk chart (data from tonelight_project) {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="ks-spectral">
      <div class="ks-spectral__head">
        <h3 class="tw:text-[20px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">The spectral picture</h3>
        <p class="ks-spectral__sub">Toggle each set of curves. Camera channels overlap and spill under white light; narrowband RGB peaks land cleanly where each channel dominates — matching the film's dye layers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ks-spectral__chips">
        <button type="button" class="ks-spectral__chip is-active" data-group="sensor" aria-pressed="true"><span class="ks-spectral__sw" style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,#5a9cf0,#4ade80,#f2555a)"></span>Camera sensitivity</button>
        <button type="button" class="ks-spectral__chip" data-group="film" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ks-spectral__sw" style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,#facc15,#e879f9,#22d3ee)"></span>Film dye density</button>
        <button type="button" class="ks-spectral__chip" data-group="narrow" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ks-spectral__sw" style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,#5a9cf0,#4ade80,#f2555a)"></span>Narrowband RGB</button>
        <button type="button" class="ks-spectral__chip is-active" data-group="broad" aria-pressed="true"><span class="ks-spectral__sw ks-spectral__sw--dash"></span>White light</button>
      </div>
      <div class="ks-spectral__chart" role="img" aria-label="Spectral curves across wavelength — camera sensor sensitivity, film dye density, narrowband RGB and broadband white-light spectra."></div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%} benefits of RGB {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="tw:mt-[56px] tw:grid tw:grid-cols-1 tw:gap-[20px] tw:md:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-white/[0.02] tw:p-[28px]">
        <div class="tw:mb-[16px] tw:flex tw:h-[44px] tw:w-[44px] tw:items-center tw:justify-center tw:rounded-[2px] tw:bg-[#3b82f6]/15"><div class="tw:h-[18px] tw:w-[18px] tw:rounded-[2px] tw:bg-[#3b82f6]"></div></div>
        <h3 class="tw:mb-[8px] tw:text-[19px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">Colour separated at the source</h3>
        <p class="tw:text-[15px] tw:leading-[1.55] tw:text-neutral-400">Each exposure reads essentially one dye layer on its own, making any camera sensor behave like photographic paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-white/[0.02] tw:p-[28px]">
        <div class="tw:mb-[16px] tw:flex tw:h-[44px] tw:w-[44px] tw:items-center tw:justify-center tw:rounded-[2px] tw:bg-[#3b82f6]/15"><div class="tw:h-[18px] tw:w-[18px] tw:rounded-[2px] tw:bg-[#3b82f6]"></div></div>
        <h3 class="tw:mb-[8px] tw:text-[19px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">Consistent across a roll</h3>
        <p class="tw:text-[15px] tw:leading-[1.55] tw:text-neutral-400">Clean input means clean scans. Shadows stay clean, skin tones look natural, and the look doesn't drift frame to frame — set it once, apply it to the whole roll.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-white/[0.02] tw:p-[28px]">
        <div class="tw:mb-[16px] tw:flex tw:h-[44px] tw:w-[44px] tw:items-center tw:justify-center tw:rounded-[2px] tw:bg-[#3b82f6]/15"><div class="tw:h-[18px] tw:w-[18px] tw:rounded-[2px] tw:bg-[#3b82f6]"></div></div>
        <h3 class="tw:mb-[8px] tw:text-[19px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">Fast and effortless</h3>
        <p class="tw:text-[15px] tw:leading-[1.55] tw:text-neutral-400">toneLight sequences the colours, fires the shutter and advances the film; toneScan Studio merges the captures on the fly. First shot to export in a couple of minutes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {%- comment -%} ==================== THE SYSTEM (3 pillars) ==================== {%- endcomment -%}
  <section id="system" class="tw:scroll-mt-[84px] tw:border-y tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-white/[0.015]">
    <div class="tw:mx-auto tw:w-full tw:max-w-[1400px] tw:px-[15px] tw:py-[80px] tw:md:px-[50px] tw:md:py-[120px]">
      <div class="tw:mx-auto tw:max-w-[760px] tw:text-center">
        <p class="tw:mb-[16px] tw:text-[13px] tw:font-semibold tw:uppercase tw:tracking-[0.16em] tw:text-[#60a5fa]">The system</p>
        <h2 class="tw:text-[32px] tw:font-semibold tw:leading-[1.1] tw:tracking-[-0.02em] tw:text-white tw:md:text-[46px]">Three parts, built to work as one.</h2>
        <p class="tw:mx-auto tw:mt-[16px] tw:max-w-[560px] tw:text-[16px] tw:text-neutral-400">A narrowband RGB light, a motorised film carrier, and desktop software — designed together so capture, transport and conversion feel like a single tool.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tw:mt-[48px] tw:grid tw:grid-cols-1 tw:gap-[20px] tw:md:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-white/[0.02] tw:p-[28px]">
          <div class="tw:mb-[20px] tw:flex tw:aspect-[3/2] tw:items-center tw:justify-center tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-neutral-900 tw:text-[13px] tw:text-neutral-600">[ toneLight ]</div>
          <h3 class="tw:mb-[8px] tw:text-[20px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">toneLight</h3>
          <p class="tw:text-[15px] tw:leading-[1.55] tw:text-neutral-400">Narrowband RGB light source for clean colour separation on any digital camera.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-white/[0.02] tw:p-[28px]">
          <div class="tw:mb-[20px] tw:flex tw:aspect-[3/2] tw:items-center tw:justify-center tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-neutral-900 tw:text-[13px] tw:text-neutral-600">[ toneCarrier ]</div>
          <h3 class="tw:mb-[8px] tw:text-[20px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">toneCarrier</h3>
          <p class="tw:text-[15px] tw:leading-[1.55] tw:text-neutral-400">Motorised film carrier that advances even short strips quickly and precisely.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-white/[0.02] tw:p-[28px]">
          <div class="tw:mb-[20px] tw:flex tw:aspect-[3/2] tw:items-center tw:justify-center tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-neutral-900 tw:text-[13px] tw:text-neutral-600">[ toneScan Studio ]</div>
          <h3 class="tw:mb-[8px] tw:text-[20px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">toneScan Studio</h3>
          <p class="tw:text-[15px] tw:leading-[1.55] tw:text-neutral-400">Desktop software that automates capture, merges the channels and inverts.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {%- comment -%} ==================== toneLight (hardware deep-dive) ==================== {%- endcomment -%}
  <section class="tw:mx-auto tw:w-full tw:max-w-[1400px] tw:px-[15px] tw:py-[80px] tw:md:px-[50px] tw:md:py-[120px]">
    <div class="tw:grid tw:grid-cols-1 tw:items-center tw:gap-[48px] tw:md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <p class="tw:mb-[14px] tw:text-[13px] tw:font-semibold tw:uppercase tw:tracking-[0.16em] tw:text-[#60a5fa]">toneLight</p>
        <h2 class="tw:text-[28px] tw:font-semibold tw:leading-[1.15] tw:tracking-[-0.02em] tw:text-white tw:md:text-[40px]">The RGB light that does the hard part in hardware.</h2>
        <p class="tw:mt-[18px] tw:text-[17px] tw:leading-[1.65] tw:text-neutral-400">toneLight is our RGB light panel engineered specifically for scanning film with a digital camera. It achieves excellent colour separation on any camera by automatically triggering red, green and blue exposures of every frame. Shoot slides or black &amp; white? A high-quality white LED panel has you covered too.</p>
        <p class="tw:mt-[16px] tw:text-[17px] tw:leading-[1.65] tw:text-neutral-400">Large physical controls make it easy to use in the dark, and an injection-moulded enclosure stands up to years of daily use.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="tw:flex tw:aspect-[4/3] tw:w-full tw:items-center tw:justify-center tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-neutral-900 tw:text-[13px] tw:text-neutral-600">[ toneLight product shot ]</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tw:mt-[40px] tw:grid tw:grid-cols-1 tw:gap-x-[40px] tw:gap-y-[20px] tw:sm:grid-cols-2 tw:md:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="tw:flex tw:gap-[12px]"><span class="tw:mt-[7px] tw:h-[7px] tw:w-[7px] tw:shrink-0 tw:rounded-full tw:bg-[#3b82f6]"></span><div><p class="tw:text-[15px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">Purpose-built RGB illumination</p><p class="tw:text-[14px] tw:leading-[1.5] tw:text-neutral-400">Narrowband LED array for sequential trichromatic capture, coordinated with the camera automatically.</p></div></div>
      <div class="tw:flex tw:gap-[12px]"><span class="tw:mt-[7px] tw:h-[7px] tw:w-[7px] tw:shrink-0 tw:rounded-full tw:bg-[#3b82f6]"></span><div><p class="tw:flex tw:items-center tw:gap-[8px] tw:text-[15px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">White &amp; infrared too <span class="tw:rounded-[2px] tw:border tw:border-white/15 tw:px-[8px] tw:py-[1px] tw:text-[11px] tw:font-medium tw:uppercase tw:tracking-[0.08em] tw:text-neutral-500">IR soon</span></p><p class="tw:text-[14px] tw:leading-[1.5] tw:text-neutral-400">High-CRI white for slide and B&amp;W; an IR channel for dust and defect capture.</p></div></div>
      <div class="tw:flex tw:gap-[12px]"><span class="tw:mt-[7px] tw:h-[7px] tw:w-[7px] tw:shrink-0 tw:rounded-full tw:bg-[#3b82f6]"></span><div><p class="tw:text-[15px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">Capture control built in</p><p class="tw:text-[14px] tw:leading-[1.5] tw:text-neutral-400">Stepper-motor film advance, direct shutter release, and a serial API for automation.</p></div></div>
      <div class="tw:flex tw:gap-[12px]"><span class="tw:mt-[7px] tw:h-[7px] tw:w-[7px] tw:shrink-0 tw:rounded-full tw:bg-[#3b82f6]"></span><div><p class="tw:text-[15px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">Simple to run</p><p class="tw:text-[14px] tw:leading-[1.5] tw:text-neutral-400">Tactile physical controls and a clear GUI — minimal interaction once it's configured.</p></div></div>
      <div class="tw:flex tw:gap-[12px]"><span class="tw:mt-[7px] tw:h-[7px] tw:w-[7px] tw:shrink-0 tw:rounded-full tw:bg-[#3b82f6]"></span><div><p class="tw:text-[15px] tw:font-semibold tw:text-white">Built for real use</p><p class="tw:text-[14px] tw:leading-[1.5] tw:text-neutral-400">Durable injection-moulded enclosure with mounting points top and bottom for copy stands and custom rigs.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {%- comment -%} ==================== toneCarrier + motor ==================== {%- endcomment -%}
  <section class="tw:border-y tw:border-white/10 tw:bg-white/[0.015]">
    <div class="tw:mx-auto tw:w-full tw:max-w-[1400px] tw:px-[15px] tw:py-[80px] tw:md:px-[50px] tw:md:py-[120px]">
      <div class="tw:grid tw:grid-cols-1 tw:items-center tw:gap-[48px] tw:md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="tw:md:order-2">
          <p class="tw:mb-[14px] tw:text-[13px] tw:font-semibold tw:uppercase tw:tracking-[0.16em] tw:text-[#60a5fa]">toneCarrier</p>
          <h2 class="tw:text-[28px] tw:font-semibold tw:leading-[1.15] tw:tracking-[-0.02em] tw:text-white tw:md:text-[40px]">Motorised film transport, frame after frame.</h2>
          <p class="tw:mt-[18px] tw:text-[17px] tw:leading-[1.65] tw:text-neutral-400">toneCarrier keeps film flat and secure during scanning, in 35 mm and 120 formats, with a belt-drive system that smoothly advances even short strips. A quiet stepper motor makes the transport fast and precise — driven by a jog dial on toneLight for fine control, or from the toneScan Studio GUI for automated capture.</p>
